}\uc1\pard\f0\fs24 P?S/8 P\f1 TUTIL User Guide\f0\par
\f2\par
\ul\f1 1.0 Introduction\ulnone\f2\par
\par
\f1 Note: This document generally follows the conventions of the \ul\i P?S/8\par
Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available separately.  For new users unfamiliar with certain aspects of P?S/8, it may be useful to read this document or the \ul\i Glossary of Terms\ulnone\i0  below before proceeding.\par
\par
\f2 P?S/8 now includes an ASCII paper-tape interchange utility named \i\f0 P\f1 TUTIL\i0 .  As of P?S/8 Version 8Z, the current release is PTUTIL Version 1E.\par
\par
Note: PTUTIL is being distributed as an add-on to existing copies of\par
P?S/8; as such, the program may only be present on certain system release media.  Appendix A contains information on using PTUTIL on\par
P?S/8 Version 8Z systems should the program not be available within the System Programs Directory.\par
\par
PTUTIL is used to process 8-level punched paper-tapes in standard ASCII text format on systems that include the requisite hardware.\par
\par
Note: Binary paper-tapes punched in standard BIN or RIM format are handled separately by the P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  program documented separately.\par
\par
Paper-tapes may be punched from the contents of existing P?S/8 SIXBIT text files or SIXBIT text files may be created from paper-tape input characters.  The direction of transfer and choice of input (or output) paper-tape device is determined by command-line option switches passed to the program.\par
\par
\ul 1.1 Specific Hardware Requirements\ulnone\par
\par
Beyond any normal system configuration to support P?S/8, PTUTIL requires the standard high-speed reader/punch hardware (device 01/02) or the low-speed reader/punch attached to the console terminal device (03/04); suitable models include the Teletype Model 33 Automatic Send and Receive (ASR) and the Teletype Model 35 ASR.  The ASR device must conform to DEC specifications regarding reader run control.\par
\par
\ul 1.2 Program limitations\ulnone\par
\par
If the source of the paper-tape input data is the low-speed reader, special handling of certain control characters (such as \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 C\highlight0\f1 , \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 S\highlight0\f1 , \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Q\highlight0\f1  and \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 O\highlight0\f1 ) will be disabled to prevent data garbling.  As such, only devices that do not require reverse flow control can be supported.  While this will always be the case on the supported Teletype models, PTUTIL may not be able to handle certain hybrid console devices that were never supported by DEC.\page If any reader character echoing issues arise (such as the need for flow control), PTUTIL must be used in an operating mode that does not access the console output device.  Command-line option switches are available to produce a variety of console output displays during reader input; If necessary, all console output can be disabled.  However, if any of the options that echo input data on the console output device are in effect, it will not be possible to detect \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 O\highlight0\f1  (which reverses the state of character echoing). Additionally, immediate program abort by the detection of \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 C\highlight0\f1  will not be possible until reader time-out has occurred.\par
\par
None of these restrictions apply when paper-tape input is obtained from the high-speed reader; however, reader throughput can be degraded by echoing the characters on a low-speed console output device.  The console output display rate must be of sufficient speed (typically exceeding 300 characters/second) to maintain reader throughput.\par
\par
High-speed readers must maintain a minimum throughput speed to avoid slowing down to a small fraction of normal operating speed.  This situation can easily become further degraded by poor maintenance practices; many high-speed readers become misadjusted with respect to the speed threshold adjustment.  Since few applications echo reader input directly, many users are unaware of this problem.  However, all related problems can be avoided by preventing character echoing (which allows the reader to run within its higher-speed performance range).\par
\par
Note: If this situation becomes chronic, it is recommended diagnostic tests be performed on the high-speed reader station and/or interface.\par
\par
\ul 1.3 ASCII text paper-tape format\ulnone\par
\par
Fully conforming ASCII text paper-tapes contain sections as follows:\par
\par
    a) Leader Section\par
\par
\tab   The leader section consists of one or more frames with only the\par
\tab   high-order bit punched.  Many paper-tape editing programs tend\par
       to create long leader sections to assist the operator in\par
\tab   positioning \tab the paper-tape in the reader.  (Note: PTUTIL\par
  \tab   ignores the leader section when creating P?S/8 output files.\par
 \tab   The first frame with any other code starts the data section.)\par
\par
\tab   If PTUTIL is used to create ASCII text paper-tapes, the default\par
\tab   leader (and trailer) length is 100 (octal 0144) frames.  This\par
\tab   value can be overridden with the \i\f2 =xxxx\i0\f1  command-line parameter\par
 \tab   to change the length to \i\f2 xxxx\i0\f1  frames as required.  Setting the\par
\tab   parameter to \i\f2 =1023\i0\f1  will produce the longest (octal 1777)\par
\tab   leader/trailer section permitted; attempts to use larger values\par
\tab   will yield an error message.\page\tab   Note: Using the \i\f2 =0\i0\f1  parameter value will eliminate all\par
\tab   leader/trailer frames.  This may be useful when directing the\par
 \tab   output to the system console device.\par
\par
    b) Data Section\par
\par
\tab   The data section generally consists of frames punched with\par
 \tab   (nearly) any ASCII character code value permitted.  While the\par
\tab   parity bit can be ignored, the specific frame value of only\par
\tab   punching the high-order bit is not allowed in the data section\par
\tab   to avoid anomaly with leader/trailer frames.\par
\par
\tab   Most paper-tape editing programs create data section frames\par
\tab   with the high-order bit set.  This is used to create clear\par
\tab   visual distinction from binary paper-tapes (which do not punch\par
\tab   the high-order bit in the binary data section).\par
\par
\tab   When PTUTIL is used to create ASCII text paper-tapes, all\par
\tab   frames in the data section are subject to the parity option in \tab   effect.  The disposition of the high-order bit \tab will be\par
 \tab   determined by PTUTIL command-line option switches that affect\par
\tab   frame parity.  All four frame parity variants are available\par
\tab   including mark parity (where the high-order bit is always set)\par
\tab   which is used by default, space parity (where the high-order\par
\tab   bit is always clear), odd parity (where the high-order bit is\par
\tab   calculated to maintain an overall odd bit count) and even\par
\tab   parity (where the high-order bit is calculated to maintain an\par
 \tab   overall even bit count).  PTUTIL ignores the parity bit within\par
\tab   all data sections of input paper-tapes.\par
\par
\tab   Note: Using parity options other than the default mark parity\par
 \tab   will yield paper-tapes possibly unreadable due to violation of \tab   prevailing PDP-8 paper-tape standards.  These options are\par
 \tab   provided for the express purpose of creating paper-tapes for\par
 \tab   export to other systems.\par
\par
\tab   When creating P?S/8 output files, data frame parity is ignored.\par
\tab   All frames in the data section are subject to conversion to\par
\tab   P?S/8 SIXBIT text format as described in Appendix C of the\par
\tab   \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate\par
\tab   document.\par
\par
\tab   Note: At the time of this writing, the above-referenced\par
 \tab   document is being updated to describe changes to recent\par
 \tab   releases of the prevailing inter-system conversion programs\par
\tab   including PTUTIL.  The only issues under review concern the\par
\tab   disposition of the seldom used \highlight1\f2 @\highlight0\f1  and \highlight1\f2 _\highlight0\f1  characters; in previous\par
\tab   releases of \i OS8CON \i0 and related conversion programs, these\par
\tab   characters were ignored.  In future releases of these\par
\tab   conversion utilities, the characters will be converted to \highlight1\f2 #\highlight0\f1  and\par
\tab   \highlight1\f2 -\highlight0\f1  respectively.\page     c) Trailer Section\par
\par
\tab   The trailer section consists of one or more frames with only\par
       the high-order bit punched.  Certain paper-tape system programs\par
       create lengthy trailer sections to assist the operator\par
       in positioning the paper-tape in the reader.\par
\par
\tab   Note: In some instances, there are multiple data sections\par
       within a single paper-tape; the trailer of one section also\par
       serves as the leader of the next section, etc.  PTUTIL supports\par
       command-line options to enable the processing of multiple input\par
\tab   sections or multiple tapes if required.\par
\par
\tab   If PTUTIL is used to punch ASCII text paper-tapes, the default \tab   trailer length is 100 (octal 0144) frames.  This value can be\par
 \tab   overridden with the \i\f2 =xxxx\i0\f1  command-line parameter to change the \tab   trailer and (leader) length as required.\par
\par
\tab   Note: When creating P?S/8 output files, PTUTIL closes the\par
       current output file on the first occurrence of a trailer\par
       character; all trailer characters are otherwise ignored.  The\par
       current output file will also be closed if there is no further\par
       paper-tape input as determined by paper-tape reader device\par
       time-out.  As such, the trailer section is considered optional.\par
       If multiple-tape options are in effect, the reader can be\par
       restarted as necessary; the operator must reposition (or\par
       change) the paper-tape in the reader device to an appropriate\par
       leader section to allow further input during the overall\par
       conversion session.\par
\par
    d) Sentinel Character\par
\par
\tab   A final frame may be punched with the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  character code \tab   to indicate overall end of frame data.  (Note: Within the\par
 \tab   prevailing operating systems supporting this convention, the\par
 \tab   sentinel frame character code is 032 octal; the high-order\par
\tab   frame bit is cleared as the sentinel character is not part of\par
\tab   the trailer section.)\par
\par
\tab   Not all operating systems support the sentinel character frame. \tab   PTUTIL can create paper-tapes with the sentinel character\par
 \tab   suppressed by using the '/Z' command-line option switch.  It is \tab   recommended that the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  character be used in\par
\tab   conjunction with a shortened trailer section (or perhaps no\par
       trailer section).  Systems that support this convention\par
       generally expect the sentinel character (or perhaps will modify\par
       the resultant file to conform to local system standards).\page\tab   The method described above applies to (at least) the TOPS-10\par
       and OS/8 operating systems.  Other systems may use the\par
       convention of \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  termination without internal leader or\par
       trailer characters (such as the P?S/8 SHELL currently under\par
       development).\par
\par
  \tab   Judicious use of the \i\f2 =xxxx\i0\f1  command and the '/Z' command-line\par
\tab   option switch should allow accommodation of virtually any\par
 \tab   operating system requirement with regard to file termination of\par
 \tab   punched ASCII text paper-tapes.\par
\par
\ul 2.0 P?S/8 keyboard monitor command formation when invoking PTUTIL\ulnone\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supports several command prompt variations.  The prompt examples used throughout this document are analogous to those used in TOPS-10 and OS/8.  This will help facilitate comparisons between the various operating systems.  \cf2\highlight3 <SP>\cf0\highlight0 , \cf2\highlight3 ,\cf0\highlight0  and \cf2\highlight3 <HT>\cf0\highlight0  characters are freely allowed between command elements (including files within file lists) to enhance command readability and are otherwise ignored.\par
\par
Command-line formation in P?S/8 allows options to be passed to system programs in the same overall manner as OS/8 CCL commands (with a few minor variations and several unique extensions).\par
\par
\ul 2.1 Overall command flow\ulnone\par
\par
The P?S/8 keyboard monitor allows command formation in either file specification order as shown below; this allows the user to work in an intuitive environment regardless of prior experience.  Commands are generally similar to various concise command implementations found in other DEC operating systems.\par
\par
Typical command examples to invoke PTUTIL (in slightly simplified form) are structured as follows:\par
\par
    a) Create P?S/8 output files from paper-tape input.\par
\par
\tab   .(optional R or RU\{n\}) PTUTIL \cf2\highlight3 output files\cf0\highlight0  < \cf2\highlight3 option switches\par
\cf0\highlight0\par
\tab    or\par
\par
\tab    .(optional R or RU\{n\}) PTUTIL > \cf2\highlight3 output files\cf0\highlight0\i  \cf2\highlight3\i0 option switches\cf0\highlight0  \par
\par
    b) Create paper-tapes from P?S/8 input files.\par
\par
\tab   .(optional R or RU\{n\}) PTUTIL < \cf2\highlight3 input files\cf0\highlight0  \cf2\highlight3 option switches\par
\cf0\highlight0\par
\tab   or\i\par
\tab\i0\par
\tab   .(optional R or RU\{n\}) PTUTIL \cf2\highlight3 input files\cf0\highlight0  >\i  \cf2\highlight3\i0 option switches\page\cf0\highlight0 Note: Attempting to pass a mixed set of input and output files to PTUITL will cause an error message and program exit.\par
\par
Within keyboard monitor execution-class commands specifying only input files, the use of \highlight1\f2 <\highlight0\f1  and \highlight1\f2 >\highlight0\f1  characters is optional; these are often used to clarify command variations by delineating command sections while maintaining command symmetry.  The use of these characters is recommended within P?S/8 \i BATCH\i0  files to enhance readability of deferred commands.\par
\par
\ul 2.2 Command-line option switches\ulnone\par
\par
The P?S/8 command-line supports option switches passed in a manner consistent with many DEC operating systems.  Two variant forms are supported as follows:\par
\par
    a) Individual option switches each preceded by \cf2\highlight3 /\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab   /F /U /V\par
\par
    b) Grouped switches preceded by \cf2\highlight3 (\cf0\highlight0  such as:\par
\par
\tab   (FUV)\par
\par
The trailing \cf2\highlight3 )\cf0\highlight0  is optional unless needed to delimit the option group from another command section.\par
\par
\ul 2.3 Passed numeric value format\ulnone\par
\par
A single numeric value can be passed to any system program using the \i =xxxx\i0  syntax where \i xxxx\i0  is any value of up to four digits in the range of 0 through 4094 (0000 through 7776 octal).  The explicit value 4095 (7777 octal) is not allowed; however, that value is passed to the program to indicate the explicit form of =xxxx was not used within the command.\par
\par
If the =xxxx parameter is not explicitly passed to PTUTIL, the number of leader/trailer frames punched when creating paper-tape output is set to the default value of 100 (0144 octal).  Passed values larger than 1023 (1777 octal) are not allowed and will yield an error message.\par
\par
Command-line option switches (and the =xxxx parameter if required) can be placed anywhere in the command line after the program name (in this instance \i\f0 P\f1 TUTIL\i0 ) and may be stated redundantly.\par
\par
For more information about P?S/8 command-line variations, consult the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.\page\ul 3.0 P?S/8 file formats supported by PTUTIL\ulnone\line\line The PTUTIL paper-tape interchange utility supports two different P?S/8 SIXBIT text file formats:\par
\par
    a) Fixed-length files that are compatible with the Tiny File\par
\tab   System (\i TFS\i0 ) directory structure,\par
\par
    b) Extended-length files.  Note: Extended-length files are only\par
\tab   available as individual files on each logical device unit.\par
\tab   Appendix B contains additional information regarding the use\par
\tab   of extended-length files.\par
\par
\ul 3.1 TFS Directory file support in PTUTIL\ulnone\par
\par
TFS Directory files are usually created by the P?S/8 keyboard monitor using built-in text editing commands; each file is 2048 12-bit words.  In general, each file will tend to contain up to 3/4 of this capacity as six-bit bytes in the P?S/8 SIXBIT text format as described in Appendix C of the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available as a separate document.  This corresponds to about 3000 characters per TFS Directory file depending on average line length and the internal line pointer and line number data.\par
\par
Note: Each line in the file has an associated pointer to the start of the line in memory and a permanent line number.  This allows rapid file editing by the P?S/8 keyboard monitor, which includes a 2048 word edit buffer in memory to hold the entire file while performing all editing operations; file updates are not made permanent until the file is written back to the storage device.\par
\par
When PTUTIL is used to create TFS Directory output files, each file includes automatically-generated line numbers starting at 100 and incrementing by 10 for each additional line.  Files are closed when a \cf2\highlight3 <FF>\cf0\highlight0  character from the paper-tape is being processed and there is less than a defined amount of free space (for potential future editing) left in the file.  Should there actually be insufficient space to store the latest line obtained from the paper-tape input, the current file will be closed and the next output file will be opened.  The process continues until the entire paper-tape is processed (or there is an error caused by insufficient output files passed to PTUTIL).\par
\par
PTUTIL can be passed up to 17 TFS Directory output files which corresponds to about 50,000 text characters of paper-tape input from a single session.\par
\par
Note: While this might be considered a large quantity of input for practical use of ASCII text paper-tapes, P?S/8 easily handles larger text projects if necessary.  This would generally be accomplished by additional processing as documented within the \ul\i P?S/8 Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  and is beyond the scope of this document.\page An error message will be issued if insufficient output files are supplied in the command invoking PTUTIL.  As such, it is generally recommended to supply more output files than contemplated.  PTUTIL displays a summary of how many output files were actually written; any unused files can be repurposed.\par
\par
\ul 3.2 Extended-length file support in PTUTIL\ulnone\par
\par
PTUTIL optionally supports P?S/8 extended-length SIXBIT text files.  This file format is identical to the TFS Directory SIXBIT text file format in terms of internal text characters; however, no line numbers (or related information) exist within these files.  As such, individual extended-length output files can typically be created up to the storage limit on each logical device unit selected for file creation.\par
\par
Appendix B describes the past, current and future implementation of extended-length files as it applies to P?S/8 Version 8P and newer releases.\par
\par
 Note: A surviving release of P?S/8 Version 8T for TC08 DECtape is available within the standard release of the \ul\i P?S Extended\ulnone\i0  \ul\i PDP-8 Simulato\ulnone\i0 r (\i PEPS)\i0  for Windows package; this obsolete system is primarily used to show the progression of P?S/8 development over the years.\par
\par
As a practical matter, a maximum of eight extended-length files may be available for use with extended-length SIXBIT text files on specific configurations; fewer logical units supporting extended-length files will be available on more modest systems depending on hardware considerations.\par
\par
When PTUTIL is writing to extended-length output files, the current file will be closed if the latest line of paper-tape input cannot be processed without exceeding file limits.  An error message will be issued if insufficient output files were passed.  PTUTIL displays a summary of how many extended-length output files were actually created during paper-tape processing; unused files can be repurposed.\par
\par
When PTUTIL is used to create ASCII text paper-tapes, all line number information (if any) is ignored.  As such, TFS Directory and extended-length input files can be freely mixed.  PTUTIL can be passed up to 17 input files.  On sufficiently large P?S/8 configurations, this corresponds to as much as 8,000,000 paper-tape output frames (not counting leader and trailer).  (Note: For all paper-tape frame tallies and summaries, PTUTIL calculates the various paper-tape frame counts in 24-bit unsigned integer arithmetic.)\page While these numbers may at first appear to be preposterously large, one of the design goals of PTUTIL is to interface P?S/8 to external host files when used in a simulated environment such as the PEPS PDP-8 simulator package for Windows; all inter-system file interchange is via paper-tape simulation.  For the largest PDP-8 programs written to date, this should be adequate for any given project.\par
\par
Note: Huge programming projects tax all PDP-8 development systems whether running on real PDP-8 hardware or implemented via simulation.\par
\par
P?S/8 can provide superior support for large projects throughout the entire development phase.  In general, large-scale programs are intended as stand-alone bootable applications.\par
\par
Note: By and large, common PDP-8 development operating systems are unsuitable for the purpose of resource management within large application-specific systems.\par
\par
\ul 4.0 Using PTUTIL to punch paper-tapes from P?S/8 input files\ulnone\par
\par
When P?S/8 input files are passed to PTUTIL, paper-tape frames are punched on the selected output device.  Up to 17 (0021 octal) P?S/8 input files can be passed to PTUTIL; the output will be a continuous stream of paper-tape frames taken from the concatenated contents of all input files.\par
\par
Note: In this operating mode, TFS Directory and extended-length input files may be freely mixed.\par
\par
Console message prompts will generally be issued at the start and finish of the paper-tape punching session to guide the user through the process.  If the output device is the low-speed punch, no finishing message can be issued; however, the program will wait for a character to be pressed on the console keyboard when the punch should be enabled (and later disabled).  This is necessary to prevent message text from being inadvertently punched to the output tape.\par
\par
\ul 4.1 PTUTIL command-line option switches used when punching paper-tapes\ulnone\par
\par
Command-line option switches that apply when punching paper-tape output are as follows:\par
\par
\tab /A (\i Automatic mode\i0 ) All prompts for user input are eliminated.\par
\tab    The selected paper-tape punch hardware must be ready before\par
\tab    invoking PTUTIL.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Automatic mode can be useful within P?S/8 BATCH sessions\par
\tab    designed to carry out repetitive tasks.\page\tab /F The default paper-tape output device is the low-speed punch of\par
\tab    the Teletype model 33 ASR or model 35 ASR console device.  If\par
\tab    the \highlight1\f2 /F\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch is set, the \tab output device\par
\tab    is changed to the DEC standard high-speed punch (device 02).\par
\par
\tab    Note: High-speed punch support is subject to hardware time-\par
\tab    out.\par
\par
\tab    If the high-speed punch is not available, the output will be\par
\tab    redirected to the console device.  Unless the \highlight1\f2 /S\highlight0\f1  command-line\par
\tab    option switch is set (for silent-running mode), unexpected\par
\tab    console message text might be punched within the paper-tape\par
\tab    output.\par
\par
\tab /S (\i Silent-running mode\i0 ) If the \highlight1\f2 /S\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch is\par
 \tab    set, all console output is suppressed with the exception of\par
\tab    serious error messages. Verbose mode (setting the \highlight1\f2 /V\highlight0\f1  command-\par
\tab    line option switch) will be ignored.\par
\par
\tab    Silent-running mode can be useful within P?S/8 BATCH\i\par
\i0  \tab    operations that might otherwise create unwanted console\par
\tab    output.  Since P?S/8 BATCH can suppress its own console output\par
\tab    messages, a carefully crafted BATCH file can be designed to\par
\tab    punch a series of paper-tapes on the low-speed punch with all\par
\tab    unwanted messages eliminated.\par
\par
\tab /U (\i Upper-case mode\i0 ) If the \highlight1\f2 /U\highlight0\f1  option switch is set, all console\par
\tab    messages will be printed in upper-case characters.  (The\par
\tab    default is mixed upper and lower case.)\par
\par
\tab    Note: This option is only required if the console terminal\par
\tab    does not support lower-case input.\par
\par
\tab    /V (\i verbose mode\i0 ) When the \highlight1\f2 /V\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch is\par
\tab    set, certain console messages will be embellished to provide\par
\tab    additional program management guidance and/or program\par
 \tab    statistics normally suppressed.\par
\par
\tab    Note: If silent-running mode is in effect (by setting the \highlight1\f2 /S\highlight0\f1\par
 \tab    command-line option switch), verbose mode will be ignored.\par
\par
\tab /Z Setting the \highlight1\f2 /Z\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch inhibits the\par
\tab    sentinel \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  character.  This can be useful when\par
\tab    punching multiple section paper-tapes.  PTUTIL would be\par
\tab    invoked with the \highlight1\f2 /Z\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch set except for\par
\tab    the final execution step that creates the sentinel character.\par
\par
\tab    Note: When creating output oriented towards certain operating\par
\tab    systems (especially when running under the SIMH PDP-8\par
\tab    simulator), host operating system conventions might warrant\par
\tab    preventing the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  character in the punched paper-tape\par
\tab    output stream.\page\tab /0 Setting the \highlight1\f2 /0\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch enables space parity\par
\tab    generation in the data section(s) of paper-tape output\par
\tab    (overriding the default mark parity); this can be useful when\par
\par
 \tab    creating output for export to systems that require space\par
 \tab    parity format.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PTUTIL does not check parity when reading paper-tape\par
\tab    data.\par
\par
\tab /1 Setting the \highlight1\f2 /1\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch enables odd parity\par
\tab    generation in the data section(s) of paper-tape output\par
\tab    (overriding the default mark parity); this can be useful when\par
\tab    creating output for export to systems that require odd parity\par
\tab    format.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PTUTIL does not check parity when reading paper-tape\par
\tab    data.\par
\par
\tab /2 Setting the \highlight1\f2 /2\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch enables even parity\par
\tab    generation in data section(s) of paper-tape output (overriding\par
\tab    the default mark parity).  This can be useful when creating\par
\tab    output for export to systems that require even parity format.\par
\par
\tab    Note: PTUTIL does not check parity when reading paper-tape\par
\tab    data.\par
\par
\tab    When using the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter and punching paper-tape data,\par
\tab    the default length of the leader and trailer sections is 100\par
\tab    (0144 octal) frames with only the high-order bit set.  The\par
\tab    =xxxx parameter can be set to any value in the range of 0\par
\tab    through 1023 (0000 octal through 1777 octal) to override the\par
\tab    default length.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Setting \i =0\i0  will eliminate the leader and trailer\par
 \tab    sections.\par
\par
\ul 5.0 Creating P?S/8 output files from paper-tapes\ulnone\par
\par
In this operating mode, paper-tape frames will be converted into the contents of P?S/8 output files.  There are two variants to this operating mode:\par
\par
\tab a) Output is created as the contents of up to 17 TFS Directory\par
\tab    output files.  Sufficient files must be passed to avoid data \par
\tab    overflow.  The count of output files created will be available\par
\tab    in the final program summary.  Unused files are ignored and\par
\tab    can be repurposed.  This output mode is used when the '/E'\par
\tab    command-line option switch is not set.\tab\tab\page\tab b) Output is created as the contents of up to eight extended-\par
\tab    length output files.  Sufficient files must be passed to avoid\par
\tab    data overflow.\par
\par
  \tab    Note: Smaller P?S/8 configurations may only support fewer than\par
\tab    eight logical device units limiting the maximum output size.\par
\tab    Additionally, the amount of space available to an extended-\par
\tab    length file is subject to several configuration issues\par
\tab    including the underlying size of the storage device.  This\par
\tab    output mode is used when the '/E' command-line option switch\par
\tab    is set.\par
\par
\ul 5.1 PTUTIL command-line option switches used when reading paper-tapes\ulnone\par
\par
Specific command-line options that apply are as follows:\par
\par
\tab /A (\i Automatic mode\i0 ) All prompts for user input are eliminated.\par
\tab    The selected paper-tape reader hardware must be ready before\par
\tab    invoking PTUTIL.\par
\par
\tab    Automatic mode can be useful within P?S/8 BATCH sessions\par
\tab    designed to carry out repetitive tasks.\par
\par
\tab /B (\i Buffered mode\i0 ) By default, all character frames received on\par
\tab    the high-speed reader are available to PTUTIL.  If the \highlight1\f2 /B\highlight0\f1  \par
 \tab    command-line option switch is enabled, a double-buffered\par
\tab    approach is enabled.  This is used in certain PDP-8 systems on\par
\tab    the theory that the apparent final character received from the\par
\tab    reader is a junk frame which must be suppressed.  See Section\par
\tab    6.1 for more information regarding using PTUTIL when running\par
\tab    under a PDP-8 simulator.\par
\par
\tab /D (\i Display mode\i0 ) If the \highlight1\f2 /D\highlight0\f1  command-line switch is set, all\par
\tab    characters from the paper-tape data section(s) are displayed\par
\tab    as read (processed according to P?S/8 character conversion\par
\tab    rules).\par
\par
\tab    Display mode should be used sparingly as throughput may be\par
\tab    dramatically reduced.  Echoing the data section characters can\par
\tab    be helpful to ensure the intended paper-tape is being read.\par
\tab    Pressing the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 O\highlight0\f1  key will reverse the current state of\par
\tab    character echoing.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Due to hardware restrictions, \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 O\highlight0\f1  detection is\par
\tab    not available when the low-speed reader is used for paper-tape\par
\tab    input.  Program abort can only be accomplished by manually\par
\tab    disabling the low-speed reader to cause a hardware time-out.\par
 \tab    The operator can press the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 C\highlight0\f1  character to exit the\par
\tab    program.\page\tab    Future releases of PTUTIL may be capable of determining if\par
\tab    the Logical Console Overlay is in effect and addressing an\par
\tab    alternate hardware device for console input; this would allow\par
\tab    restoration of these functions controlled from the alternate\par
\tab    hardware input device.\par
\par
\tab /E (\i Extended-length file mode\i0 ) Setting the \highlight1\f2 /E\highlight0\f1  command-line switch\par
\tab    indicates the use of extended-length output files.  The\par
\tab    default operating mode uses TFS Directory output files.\par
\par
\tab /F The default paper-tape reader is the low-speed reader of the\par
\tab    Teletype model 33 ASR or model 35 ASR console device.  If the\par
\tab    \highlight1\f2 /F\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch is set, the input device is\par
\tab    changed to the DEC standard high-speed reader (device 01).\par
