\par
\tab /V (\i verbose mode\i0 ) When the \highlight1\f2 /V\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch is set,\par
\tab    certain console messages will be embellished to provide\par
\tab    additional program management guidance and/or program\par
\tab    statistics normally suppressed.\par
\par
\tab    Note: If silent-running mode is in effect (by setting the \highlight1\f2 /S\par
\highlight0\f1\tab    command-line option switch), verbose mode will be ignored.\par
\par
\tab /X When the \highlight1\f2 /X\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch is set, special\par
\tab    handling of the \cf2\highlight3 <FF>\cf0\highlight0  character is disabled; the \cf2\highlight3 <FF>\cf0\highlight0  character\par
\tab    will be converted to a P?S/8 end-of-line sequence.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Setting the \highlight1\f2 /X\highlight0\f1  command-line option switch causes the \highlight1\f2 /Q\highlight0\f1   \tab    command-line option switch to be ignored.\page\ul 6.0 SIMH simulator and related issues\ulnone\line\line When P?S/8 is run under a PDP-8 simulator, host system file conventions may influence the preferred command-line options used when running PTUTIL.\par
\par
\ul 6.1 High-speed reader considerations\ulnone\par
\par
MS-DOS and Windows-based simulators use file systems that maintain a byte count for each file.  As such, there is no sentinel \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  character requirement; files maintained in the host system cannot be expected to include the sentinel character.  When these files are passed to P?S/8, the end of input will of necessity be obtained by high-speed reader time-out.\par
\par
Certain PDP-8 programs (including the standard high-speed reader device handler within OS/8) use double-buffering of the input frames expecting the final frame detected to be junk data; the last character frame will not be returned to the calling program.  As such, in OS/8, the last character of the simulated high-speed reader input is never made available.\par
\par
The default operation of PTUTIL is to allow every character detected to be available as an input character frame.  Enabling double-buffering with the '/B' command-line option switch will prevent PTUTIL from processing the last character frame and is generally not recommended.\par
\par
\ul 6.2 Host file sentinel character considerations\ulnone\par
\par
When punching paper-tape output, a designated host file stores all frames sent; if PTUTIL is running under the Windows file system, all files are maintained with exact byte counts; as such, PTUTIL may be run with the '/Z' command-line switch set to suppress the \highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  sentinel character.  (Note: Depending on program specifics, the\par
\highlight1\f2 Control\f1\fs29 -\f2\fs24 Z\highlight0\f1  sentinel character may appear as an unwanted artifact of the punching operation when performing host editing operations.)\par
\par
When running PTUTIL under other host operating systems, the exact output file requirement can be adjusted for local host file system conventions as required.\par
\par
\ul 6.3 Parity bit issues\ulnone\line\line It may be necessary to use the '/0' command-line option switch to enable space parity in all output data frames.  Many systems support extensions to the seven-bit ASCII character set; as such, setting the high-order bit may cause misinterpretation as an extended character instead of the intended seven-bit ASCII value.  This could include leader/trailer characters which can be suppressed as necessary using the \i =0\i0  parameter setting.\page Note: Information on the various SIMH simulator implementations is available elsewhere as part of various SIMH document collections.\par
\par
\ul 7.0 Additional PEPS for Windows considerations\ulnone\par
\par
As discussed in Section 6 above, it is possible there can be artifacts remaining in files transmitted to the Windows environment as punched paper-tape character streams.\line\line In the PEPS for Windows package, commands are available to open and/or edit these files; artifact problems present are generally immediately obvious.  To eliminate any and all such problems, the PEPS package includes a file-stripping utility that removes all known artifacts from either the high-speed punch output or the device 66 lineprinter output.\line\line As such, the file-stripping utility can be used to eliminate all problems within ASCII text files.\par
\par
Note: The stripping utility can also eliminate problems with files obtained externally from such as unix systems and/or Internet sources.  Once stripped, text files conform to PDP-8 conventions and can be sent to the simulated PDP-8 environment with all attendant problems removed.\par
\par
Additional information on the PEPS for Windows package version of the SIMH PDP-8 simulator is available from the author of both this document and the PEPS package: Charles Lasner.\page\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\par
Appendix A - Using PTUTIL without full installation\par
\par
PTUTIL is a recent addition to later releases of P/S/8 Version 8Z; it will be included in all future releases as a standard system program. \par
\par
Users familiar with the P?S/8 System Programs Directory can add PTUTIL to existing systems where PTUTIL is not installed (assuming sufficient free blocks are available).  PTUTIL can also be run as an impromptu system program as described below; the binary files created by assembling the PTUTIL source files must be available on an accessible logical device unit.\par
\par
Note: Logical unit 1 is used in this example; appropriate changes can be applied as needed.\par
\par
