\par
PTUTIL has been designed to be (nearly) compatible with the technique described above to allow quick addition to existing systems; this execution mechanism alternative was introduced in P?S/8 Version 8Y and is supported on all newer releases.  The majority of conforming system programs can be supported using this tentative mechanism in lieu of inclusion within the System Programs Directory (unless overlay structures are required).\page\cf3 Appendix A (continued)\par
\cf0\par
Usage restrictions.\par
\par
When using PTUTIL to create ASCII text paper-tape output, the default leader/trailer length (surrounding the content obtained from all input files) is 100 (0144 octal) frames.  This value can be changed using the \i =xxxx\i0  parameter which is normally expressed in the current keyboard monitor radix (which is generally decimal).  However, the execution attribute to maintain the current radix is only available to programs located in the System Programs Directory.  (The default value is to force octal radix).  As such, any usage of the =xxxx parameter as described here must be expressed in octal.\par
\par
End of Appendix A\page\f1\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\par
Appendix B - Extended-length file support in P?S/8 Version 8Z\par
\par
Some form of extended-length file support has (nearly) always existed within P?S/8.  The internal file format (described elsewhere) is ASCII \line SIXBIT text.  These files are created by PTUTIL and other inter-system conversion utilities (such as \i OS8CON\i0  and \i L6DCON\i0 ); however, the associated directory structure has gone through several partially functional (and incompatible) implementations over time.  The current structure will be scrapped and logically replaced with the P?S/8 SHELL (which is expected to be an integral component of P?S/8 Version 9B).\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 Version 9A (the next designated version) will largely be a cleanup release to remedy a number of issues that have been discovered over time.  Small changes will be made to many programs to achieve internal improvement.  From a usage standpoint, little will be changed with the exception of the specifics of extended-length files access.\par
\par
At present, P?S/8 Version 8Z includes the vestigial Extended-length File Directory structure that only provides the rudiments of extended-length file support with serious issues as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) Extended-length filenames are currently not supported.\par
\tab    Instead, an existing TFS Directory file must be specified\par
\tab    which is the highest (by storage block number) within the TFS\par
\tab    Directory.\par
\par
\tab    Admittedly, this implementation is seriously flawed.  Users\par
\tab    must be aware of and comply with this restriction; improper\par
\tab    usage (without any proper safeguards) can easily destroy files\par
\tab    further up the device (residing on higher storage blocks).\par
\tab    The proper filename to provide (in P?S/8 Version 8Z) is the\par
\tab    last filename displayed by the CA\{TALOG\} command output on the \tab    system console.\par
\par
\tab b) The Extended-length Files Directory structure currently only\par
\tab    serves a single purpose, which is to establish the high limit\par
\tab    block on each logical unit.  This value is used to ensure\par
\tab    extended-length file access is limited to intended blocks.\par
\par
\tab c) The only way to rename extended-length files is manually\par
\tab    patching the filename characters (using P?S/8 \i DUMP\i0  or \i BLKODT\i0 ). \par
\par
\tab    Note: P?S/8 Version 8T (and similar releases) supports the\par
\tab    first filename in the Extended-length Files Directory.\par
\tab    However, the limit block is determined by the console switch\par
\tab    register while running \i BIGCON\i0  (a forerunner of \i OS8CON\i0 ).  The\par
\tab    current implementation in P?S/8 Version 8Z is a stop-gap\par
\tab    improvement until a proper remedy is released.\page\cf3 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab d) The rest of the Extended-length Files Directory structure is\par
\tab    currently unused.  It was originally created for a theoretical\par
\tab    attempt at a multiple-file directory structure that was never\par
\tab    fully implemented.  This has since been scrapped in favor of\par
\tab    the P?S/8 SHELL which is currently under development.\par
\cf3\par
\cf0 As such, utilities such as PTUTIL check to see if the '/E' command-line option switch is set; however, no validation can be performed to determine if output file handles reference extended-length file blocks.  As described above, this is currently impossible; the passed block value merely shows the presumed highest block(s) actively used in the TFS Directory.  Files will be created from the passed block value through as much as all the rest of the blocks on the storage device as indicated in the Extended-length Files Directory.  Thus, the user is warned to always pass the highest actual filename in the TFS Directory to avoid file destruction.\par
\par
All analogous conversion programs that create P?S/8 extended-length output files reserve internal initialization code space.  When feasible, the space will be used for future implementation where extended-length output files are confirmed to start outside of the scope of the TFS Directory; appropriate validation can be applied (including appropriate error messages).  When this is operational, the temporary kludge of supplying the starting block of the highest-block existing TFS Directory file will cease to function; safe and proper support of the individual extended-length file will replace the current risky implementation.\cf3\par
\par
\cf0 The Extended-length Files Directory block structure will be removed from P?S/8 (ultimately to be repurposed.)  This will be replaced by a mechanism that directly defines the block limits of the individual extended-length file within the System Programs Directory.\par
\par
In future P?S/8 releases, it will be necessary to use the R (or RU\{N\}) form of program execution to access extended-length files.  This is in keeping with the original intention of P?S/8 extended-length file support; all proposed support mechanisms require sufficient code space in memory that cannot coexist with the keyboard monitor edit buffer.  \par
\par
Note: The keyboard monitor provides support routines for the execution-class code sections that are brought into memory, including the concise execution mechanism that overlays a portion of the keyboard monitor.  This overall scheme is especially useful to certain utility programs that interact directly with the keyboard monitor in various ways.  Certain programs are designed to post-process the command line for additional information; a good example is the P?S/8 \i SET\i0  utility.  The entry for the \i SET\i0  program in the System Programs Directory indicates limited parsing by the execution-class command processor to allow the \i SET\i0  utility to process commands via its own syntax parsing routines.\page\cf3 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
Historically, in all relevant implementations of P?S/8, a distinction has always been made between supporting the first physical half of the System Programs Directory (the first two blocks) and supporting the entire directory due to specific resource issues.\par
\par
The concise form of program execution (where merely the program name is invoked causing it to be executed) restricts the entry for the target program to be located within the first half of the System Programs Directory.  This allows the P?S8 keyboard monitor to overlay the code that supports the execution and the first half of the System Programs Directory over a portion of the keyboard monitor (containing routines that are not used during the command-line processing).\par
\par
Note: Where relevant, the displaced portion of the keyboard monitor will be restored by the target program after the command is processed. This also occurs if there was a program lookup failure or other command parsing problem.  This is important in the case of programs such as the \i FIND\i0  and \i CHANGE\i0  utilities which interact with the keyboard monitor and the edit buffer.  Most program execution commands merely lead to loading a program image into some portion of user memory (locations 00000-07577) which ultimately exits to location 07600 to reload the keyboard monitor; generally, no further interaction with the keyboard monitor is required unless the program is performing an abort operation.\par
\par
The advantage of this form of execution is the preservation of the edit buffer which is crucial to the execution of certain overlaying utilities (including P?S/8 \i BATCH\i0 ) which interact with the edit buffer contents.  When PTUTIL is invoked without extended-length output files, this is a suitable means for initiating the utility.\par
\par
One reason for using the R (or RU\{N\}) form of execution is to allow system program names that are anomalous to keyboard monitor commands.  Additionally, since the second half of the system programs directory is fully accessible, this allows an upper limit of over 80 system programs; the concise execution form limit is 42 system programs (as stored in the first half of the System Programs Directory).\par
\par
All program entries contained in the second half of the Systems Program Directory must, of necessity, not depend on the preservation of the edit buffer.\par
\par
Note: This is generally the case, considering how few programs actually require edit buffer preservation.  For most system programs, the lower overhead of the concise form of execution is a welcome improvement in performance; however, this is not an actual requirement.\page\cf3 Appendix B (continued)\par
\par
\cf0 As a practical matter, no existent P?S/8 release has ever fully occupied the first half of the System Programs Directory; the typical overall system program count is 30 (or less) unique entries.  (Certain entries are merely aliases to other programs in the System Programs Directory.)  This has partially been a result of ensuring most system program names are not in conflict with the keyboard monitor commands. For the average user, there are few (if any) additional programs that warrant placement in the System Programs Directory.\par
\par
Generally, most programs are executed as TFS binary files loaded by the \i BIN\i0  utility.  As such, there haven't been enough system programs to justify the full length of the System Programs Directory.\par
\par
Note: Variant system releases can make these system programs entries meaningful; for example, numerous diagnostic program images could be added to a customized P?S/8 release.\par
\cf3\par
\cf0 Thus, it has always been intended to require these restrictions to access extended-length output files (as well as the entire System Programs Directory).  The edit buffer memory locations are overlaid; a complete keyboard monitor reload is required if various errors are detected while parsing the command.  Until the solution described below is implemented, the workaround for P?S/8 Version 8Z (as described above) must be used to access extended-length files.\par
\par
Extended-length files will be formally redefined in future releases of P?S/8 starting with Version 9A.  Each logical unit will support one extended-length file; supported file formats will not change.  As a practical matter, the extended-length file starting block will be exactly 16 (0020 octal) blocks past the best-case workaround of supplying the highest possible TFS Directory filename (assuming the TFS Directory is full).\par
\par
Note: By design, the extended-length file storage follows the highest file permitted in the TFS Directory.  The present workaround actually allows 16 (0020 octal) additional blocks for the largest possible extended-length file as compared to the eventual formal redefinition.  However, the extent of the TFS Directory can always be adjusted downward to allow more storage allocated to the extended-length file on any specific logical device unit.  If desired, the TFS Directory on the device unit can be rendered moot causing all available storage past the last block reserved by the System Programs Directory to be available for the extended-length file.  P?S/8 storage devices such as DECtapes can be configured in this manner for routine use with maximum size extended-length files.  This is routinely used with programs such as P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  to process very large source files.\page\cf3 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
Details of the future implementation of extended-length file support include repurposing a few words at the end of the System Programs Directory for the extended-length file parameters.  This will have minimal impact on the overall capacity of the System Programs Directory; in the worst case planned implementation, three less slots will be available for additional system programs (leaving a maximum of 82 program entries).  As a practical matter, the System Programs Directory is presently utilized at under 40% of capacity; as such, this should be of no particular consequence for any intended usage.\par
\par
Since the extended length file entry will be located in the System Programs Directory, it will have already been read in with the rest of the system programs support structure when the R (or RU\{N\}) form of execution is invoked.  (The blocks overlaying a portion of the edit buffer include the few pages of code to process the entire command and the entirety of the System Programs Directory, which are located on adjacent blocks; as such, a single read operation sets up the entire command parsing process.)\par
\par
Some of the underlying blocks currently assigned to the Extended-length Files Directory structure will be repurposed into part of a new low-level keyboard monitor used to manage all structures (such as the Logical Console Overlay and the P?S/8 SHELL) that wrest control from the keyboard monitor.  This situation can become undesirable for a variety of reasons.  For example, if the Logical Console Overlay is in control on a dismountable system, a serious problem could result should the system media be moved to a different machine lacking the key hardware.  Additional blocks may be repurposed to other modules of the P?S/8 SHELL implementation such as the virtualized copy of the File and Communications area.\par
\par
Once this updated form of program execution is fully implemented, all programs such as PTUTIL will be revised to fully support extended-length files with proper validation (meaning confirmation of starting file blocks required to exist beyond those within the TFS Directory structure that precedes it on the same logical device).  In future releases, PTUTIL (and other conversion utilities) can check the TFS Directory structure on the same logical unit the extended-length file entry passed resides; the limit block of the TFS Directory defines the lowest block allowed for extended-length files.  Thus, the passed file handle parameter should match this value.\par
\par
Access to the dedicated entry in each system programs directory for the single extended-length file will require the user to set an appropriate unique filename not anomalous to any file within the associated TFS Directory on the same logical unit.  If there is sufficient code space, the execution code will implement safeguards to ensure unique filenames by attempting to lookup the same filename within the associated TFS Directory.\page\cf3 Appendix B (continued)\par
\cf0\par
Renaming of extended-length files will be implemented using new \i SET\i0  command options to establish proper naming conventions as well as preventing filename anomaly.  Additional \i SET\i0  command options will manage scratch extended-length file area lists for programs such as\par
P?S/8 PAL, which require large temporary files during the operation of the cross-reference module.  (Up to eight extended-length files can be made available for reference storage during the assembly; active extended-length files resident on particular logical units must be excluded from this list to avoid destruction.)\par
\par
End of Appendix B\page\f1\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\f2\par
Glossary of Terms\par
\par
1. Edit buffer\par
\par
   TFS text files are created in the edit buffer using commands of the\par
   keyboard monitor.  Files are tentative until the edit buffer\par
   contents are saved to a file in the TFS Directory.\par
\par
2. Extended-length text files\par
\par
   Extended-length files partially resemble TFS text files except no\par
   actual line number or line pointer data exists.  Additionally,\par
   there are no length restrictions on extended-length files (other\par
   than the physical limits of the device).\par
\par
3. Extended-length Files Directory\par
\par
   This structure (which was never completed) is being removed in the\par
   next release of P?S/8.  The original intention was to support\par
   multiple extended-length files; however this design was abandoned.\par
   Future releases will include the P?S/8 SHELL (currently under\par
   development).  In P?S/8 Version 8Z, the only purpose of this\par
   structure is to establish the highest block that an extended-length\par
   file may write to.\par
\par
   In P?S/8 Version 8Z the system programs (such as \i PTUTIL\i0 , \i OS8CON\i0  and\par
   \i L6DCON\i0  that support writing extended-length files) are passed the\par
   starting block of extended-length files by a potentially dangerous\par
   kludge.  For each such file, the starting block is specified by a\par
   TFS file that must be positioned high enough in the TFS Directory\par
   to prevent destruction of any TFS files potentially stored further\par
   up the device.  The next release will eliminate this problem by\par
   specifying a single extended-length file explicitly using a special\par
   entry within the System Programs Directory; since the extended-\par
   length file can only be accessed via the R (or RU\{N\}) command\par
   (which reads in the entire System Programs Directory as well as the\par
   command processing code), overall system performance will actually\par
   improve when all related modifications are implemented.\par
\par
4. Logical Console Overlay\par
\par
   The Logical Console Overlay is an optional component of P?S/8.\par
   When enabled, all conforming programs will direct all system \par
   console (and lineprinter) input/output to designated memory\par
   locations instead of the usual physical device.  While the use of\par
   the Logical Console Overlay requires the use of extended memory,\par
   certain advanced features are enabled as follows:\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0    \par
\par
   1) If the P?S/8 system device handler detects an error, the usual\par
      action is to halt the CPU (possibly displaying status bits in\par
      the accumulator); pressing \highlight1 CONTINUE\highlight0  on the system front panel\par
      will safely retry the latest read/write operation.  Users need\par
      to be familiar with the particular system device handler at hand\par
      to gain insight as to what caused the specific problem (if\par
      possible) by examining the contents of the accumulator.\par
\par
      Note: An available system option is to disable the system halt\par
      which results in an infinite retry of all errors; depending on\par
      hardware particulars, this setting may not be recommended.\par
\par
      When the Logical Console Overlay is enabled, the action taken on\par
      detection of an error by the system device handler becomes a\par
      status report printed on the system console device (as defined\par
      by the Logical Console Overlay which could include alternate\par
      console hardware).  This includes details of the latest\par
      read/write operation; the user can choose which action to take\par
      at the point of error by entering one of the stated options on\par
      the system console keyboard.  This allows the user to correct\par
      the problem (if possible) and retry the latest read/write\par
      operation, or to abort the latest operation (if necessary).\par
      Without requiring specific knowledge of the vagaries of the\par
      current system device, any user can properly manage the system\par
 \tab  device operations.\par
\par
   2) Not every system device handler is capable of maximal\par
      performance; this is especially true of system device handlers\par
      designed to run on 4K systems (without requiring loading of a\par
      portion of the handler into extended memory).  Due to limited\par
      memory space, it is often necessary to compromise performance to\par
      obtain basic functionality.\par
\par
      When the Logical Console Overlay is enabled, additional\par
      functionality (included in the system device handler for this\par
      purpose) is also enabled.  This generally effects additional\par
      performance that overcomes the basic design compromise.\par
\par
      Note: On hardware configurations where the system device handler\par
      requires an extended-memory component, the Logical Console\par
      Overlay is designed to function in the rest of the same memory\par
\tab  field.  As such, for conforming programs attempting to locate\par
      extended memory fields, the use of the Logical Console Overlay\par
      does not change the number of complete memory fields available.\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
   3) Certain configurations of the Logical Console Overlay may use\par
 \tab  alternate console hardware (such as an alternate console\par
 \tab  terminal with device codes differing from 03/04).  Future\par
\tab  releases of PTUTIL will be able to detect the presence of these\par
\tab  configurations; this will allow full message control during\par
\tab  PTUTIL operations even if the low-speed reader/punch is\par
 \tab  selected.\par
\par
5. System Programs Directory\par
\par
   In the basic P?S/8 system, the System Programs Directory structure\par
   is independent of all other directories.  Every system program is\par
   stored in system blocks located prior to user files.  Execution-\par
   class commands access the System Programs Directory to establish\par
   the memory loading/starting parameters for the specified program.\par
   Certain programs specify other attributes needed to perform special\par
   parsing rules that may apply.  Every logical unit includes its own\par
   System Programs Directory; as such, in a multiple logical unit\par
   system configuration, numerous system programs may be available.\par
 \par
   All system programs can specify additional attributes as follows:\par
\par
   1) Parsing of all files can be inhibited.  This is required by\par
      programs such as \i SET\i0  which perform special parsing of the rest\par
      of the command line unrelated to filenames.\par
\par
   2) Parsing of input files can be inhibited.  This is required by\par
      programs such as \i BSAVE\i0  which require output files while parsing\par
      the rest of the command line as memory ranges to save.\line\line    3) The \i =xxxx\i0  is normally processed as an octal value.  If required,\par
      this can be forced to use the current radix in effect.  This is\par
\tab  useful for programs such as the \i FIND\i0  utility which interacts\par
\tab  with the edit buffer using file line number values.\par
\par
   The System Programs Directory will be redesigned with the release\par
   of P?S/8 Version 9A.  The new release will include explicit\par
   information on the extended-length file located past the TFS\par
   directory on the same logical unit.\par
\par
   Prior releases use the incomplete Extended-length Files Directory\par
   to determine the highest block allowed for writing to the extended-\par
   length file.  This obsolescent structure was intended to support\par
   multiple files; however, it was never fully implemented.  Future\par
   releases of P?S/8 will support the new P?S/8 SHELL structure\par
   (currently under development).  The P?S/8 SHELL will support\par
   storage blocks past the extended-length file.  (Note: The capacity\par
   of any of these structures can be adjusted downward to any\par
   storage size including zero; as such, it is possible to effectively\par
   have a device with only the SHELL structure active.)\page\cf3 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
6. Tiny File System (TFS) Directory\par
\par
   This is the primary structure for user files in the basic P?S/8\par
   system.  Each TFS file is of fixed size allowing directory slots\par
   that cannot overlap other files in the overall structure.  Projects\par
   can extend across multiple TFS files; the contents of all specified\par
   files will be logically concatenated together when processed by\par
   most system programs (such as P?S/8 \i PAL\i0 ).  Programs that create\par
   output files are aware of the implied file size (with the exception\par
   of certain programs such as \i PTUTIL\i0 , \i OS8CON\i0  and \i L6DCON\i0  which can\par
   optionally create extended-length files).\line\line    There are unique advantages to the TFS structure which, for certain\par
   users, requires some measure of orientation and experience.  Once\par
   mastered, rapid editing of large projects can proceed with little\par
   additional effort.\par
\par
   Note: All P?S/8 system programs that create TFS files are aware of\par
   the specifics of the file structure; this includes binary files as\par
   created by P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  and P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  (when creating binary files from\par
   binary paper-tape input data).\cf3\par
\cf0\par
7. Tiny File System (TFS) text files.\par
\par
   Text files created by the keyboard monitor are of fixed size.  The\par
   lower addresses of the file store the actual text characters\par
   expanding towards the rear of the file.  Line pointer and line\par
   number data are stored at the higher addresses within the file,\par
   expanding towards the front of the file.  (The file is full when an\par
   attempt to add an additional text line would overflow the file\par
   while within the edit buffer; the actual file on a device is not\par
   affected until the edit buffer is saved to an actual TFS file.)\par
\par
   A system program that unpacks the text characters ignored the line\par
   pointer and line number values; the text processed is the\par
   concatenation of all files passed to the program in the order\par
   specified by the user within an execution-class command.\par
\par
End of Glossary of Terms\par
